T7.0 attempt A7 - "N+1 regression", as a code change to product-catalog. Apply inside world-v2/:

    cd world-v2 && git apply ../evals/attempts/product-catalog-n-plus-one.patch

ListProducts loads every product in one query. This makes it re-fetch each product individually
afterwards - the N+1 shape - thirty times per product, so ten products cost ~300 one-millisecond
queries and the p95 crosses the 250 ms rule. The multiplier is chosen to cross the threshold and
is stated as such; what is under test is the MECHANISM (a deployed code change reaches the world
only by swapping the image), not the number.

diff --git a/src/product-catalog/main.go b/src/product-catalog/main.go
index 05ad597..7fb24ab 100644
--- a/src/product-catalog/main.go
+++ b/src/product-catalog/main.go
@@ -423,6 +423,17 @@ func (p *productCatalog) ListProducts(ctx context.Context, req *pb.Empty) (*pb.L
 		return nil, status.Errorf(codes.Internal, "failed to load products: %v", err)
 	}
 
+	// T7.0 attempt A7: the N+1 regression. Re-read every product one at a time after the list
+	// query that already returned them, thirty times each, so the cost is visible at p95.
+	for _, product := range products {
+		for i := 0; i < 30; i++ {
+			if _, err := getProductFromDB(ctx, product.Id); err != nil {
+				span.SetStatus(otelcodes.Error, err.Error())
+				return nil, status.Errorf(codes.Internal, "failed to re-read product %s: %v", product.Id, err)
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	span.SetAttributes(
 		attribute.Int("app.products.count", len(products)),
 	)
